we climb in nicely if feels very smooth  over James Ketchel likes a challenge but  even by his own admission flying solo  around the world in a gyrocopter  there's his toughest to date the serial  adventurer took off from Popham airfield  in Hampshire and March the 31st and now  70 days later he's halfway through his  epic journey reaching Nome in Alaska  he's flown over some of the most remote  and coldest places on earth there's no  real room for error you've got to get it  right but if you wait for the weather  it's it's not too bad  my djaro is an open cockpit gyro so it's  cold and that's that's one of the really  cold and that's one of the challenges  that that I have but it was never gonna  be easy Ketchel needs to fly 37,000  kilometers to achieve his ambition of  becoming the first person to  circumnavigate the globe solo in a  gyrocopter his flight path takes him  across Europe into Asia up into North  America and the Arctic Circle and  finally back home to England a small  aircraft travels at 70 knots and has a  range of 700 miles as the gyrocopters  are amazing you can't stall the  gyrocopter and they've been around for a  long time but you know typically people  don't fly them around the world I wanted  to sort of prove that actually these  aircraft are a lot more capable then  than people might think this latest  record breaking attempt follows quetzals  ultimate triathlon which saw him row  across the Atlantic Ocean in 2010 climb  Everest in 2011 and cycle 18,000 miles  around the world in 2013  the air building in sight it came all  the way he's described this mission as  his biggest adventure so far and hopes  it will help inspire and motivate young  people around the world to follow their  own goals as well as raising money for  charity  Ashish Joshi sky news
 

